  "body": "Makes a lathe holder body reachable: the tool editor and the MCP server can now give a turning tool a holder from a STEP, STL or OBJ file, placed in the turning tool frame. The judging law (#369) and its insert zone (#396) already existed, but nothing a user could reach led to them.\n\nFork synced at v0.18.0 (branch cut from upstream/main 3e9b8ae1).\n\n## Where #361 stood\n\nThe triage split (#368, #369, #370, #371) has all shipped. What was missing is the case the issue names first, asymmetric lathe tooling:\n\n- The tool editor placed every body along a spindle axis, so Apply body on a turning tool was refused with \"lathe holders are placed in the turning tool frame\" and the GUI had no way to do that.\n- The MCP server had no tool-body tool; the camcraft-mcp skill listed it as a known gap.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **Tool editor.** On a lathe-held tool the Body section places the imported file in the turning tool frame: radial and spindle directions (a preset or a picked face normal), the tip (numbers or a picked corner) and an optional insert zone. Apply body queues the existing `SetToolBody::lathe`, one undo step. A boring bar, which serves both machines, gets a frame switch. Milling tools are unchanged.\n- **MCP.** Five tools in `crates/app/src/mcp/body_tools.rs`: `get_tool_body`, `set_tool_body` (by numbers, or by a STEP's ISO 13399 coordinate systems), `set_lathe_tool_body`, `set_lathe_insert_zone`, `clear_tool_body`. Each write is one `SetToolBody`.\n- **Safety: a reduced-fidelity STEP is refused as a tool body.** Every body-file path used `load_model`, which drops #864's chord error. A coarsely meshed holder reads smaller than the holder is, so its collision checks were too generous with nothing said. All body files now pass one door (`tool_body_file`), which refuses such a file by name and says to simplify the model. This touches only the tool-body readers, not the part laws #904 is working on.\n- **Process.** The camcraft-mcp skill lists the new tools and drops the gap; `PROCESS_VERSION` 16 -> 17.\n- **Fixture.** `testdata/models/lathe_holder_offset.step` is our own extruded L (an offset holder), written by a small script. No vendor file was read or committed.\n\nNo new judging law and no change to posted output for existing documents. Decisions are in `docs/decisions/361-vendor-tool-models.md`.\n\n## How tested\n\nQuick gate (`cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main`) green on 1a6d4017: 26 app suites and the ui lib plus 10 ui suites, 0 failed. Also run and green: `tool_body`, `turning_holder`, `tool_csys`, `turning_insert_zone`, `holder_library`, `tool_body_judged`, `tool_body_bands`, and `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui --all-targets -- -D warnings`.\n\nPinning tests:\n\n- `crates/app/tests/vendor_tool_body.rs`\n  - `a_step_lathe_holder_placed_off_its_model_frame_is_judged_and_its_mirror_stands_clear`: the STEP holder rubs the part's shoulder (3.2 mm short, post blocked); the same file with the spindle direction reversed is clear.\n  - `a_step_meshed_coarser_than_promised_is_refused_as_a_tool_body_by_name`: all five file doors refuse.\n  - `the_lathe_placement_preview_is_the_commands_own_reading`\n- `crates/app/tests/mcp_tool_body.rs` (5 tests): both frames, the insert zone, undo/redo, and every refusal by name.\n- `crates/ui/src/egui_app/tests/tool_body_lathe.rs` (6 tests): Apply body lands one undoable command, a non-square placement and an oversized zone are refused by name, picks fill only the draft, the boring bar's frame switch, the coarse-STEP refusal at import.\n\nI also rendered the editor once at 1280x720 to check the lathe section fits; no snapshot PNG is changed.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- No real vendor STEP was tried (none may be committed, and none was used); the fixture is eight planar faces.\n- The picks were tested through the state calls, not by clicking the 3D preview.\n- No hands-on run of the GUI beyond the one rendered frame.\n- Inch documents: the lathe fields use the same unit lens as the other fields, but no test types an inch value into them.\n\n## Still owed on #361\n\n- #1037 (P1): the lathe holder body is judged against part and stock only, not workholding or fixtures.\n- #1038: the lathe body is not drawn in the simulation or viewport.\n- #1039: a lathe placement from a file's ISO 13399 coordinate systems.\n- #1040: a tool assembled from several vendor bodies (the ISO 26622 / 26623 interfaces the issue names).\n- #1041: judge a reduced-fidelity body grown by its chord error instead of refusing it.\n- Imported bodies in `.camtools` library files ride #889.\n\nChangelog: Lathe holder bodies from the tool editor and MCP: a turning tool's STEP holder is placed in the turning tool frame (directions, tip, insert zone); MCP `get_tool_body`, `set_tool_body`, `set_lathe_tool_body`, `set_lathe_insert_zone`, `clear_tool_body`; a STEP too detailed to mesh at full fidelity is refused as a tool body (#361)\n\nEffort: max (not escalated)\n\nRefs #361\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
